# Legal drafting voice

Legal documents should read like they were drafted by a top-tier business law firm: formal, precise, comprehensive, and calm. Never name or allude to outside counsel, law firms, or who drafted the document.

This rule overrides `draper-honeycopy.mdc` on legal surfaces. Marketing voice does not belong in legal text.

## Tone

- Authoritative and measured. No hype, wit, or sales language.
- Clear plain English where the audience is a consumer. Formal precision where the audience is a business counterparty.
- Neutral third person or defined-party voice. Avoid conversational filler.
- Risk-aware. State obligations, limits, and remedies explicitly. Do not overpromise.

## Drafting conventions

| Element | Standard |
| --- | --- |
| Defined terms | Introduce in quotes on first use, then capitalize consistently (`"Service"`, `"Customer"`, `"Effective Date"`) |
| Obligations | `shall` for binding duties; `may` for permission; avoid vague "should" in operative sections |
| Structure | Numbered sections and subsections; Definitions up front for agreements; topical headings for policies |
| Cross-references | `as set forth in Section 4.2`, `pursuant to this Agreement` |
| Lists | Parallel grammar; complete sentences when each item is a standalone obligation |

## Standard agreement architecture

Include as applicable:
- title, parties, effective date
- recitals (`WHEREAS` / `NOW, THEREFORE`) for contracts
- definitions
- operative terms (license, fees, term, termination, confidentiality, IP, warranties, liability, indemnity)
- general provisions (governing law, venue, notices, assignment, severability, entire agreement, amendments, waiver, force majeure)
- signature block

## Policy documents (Terms, Privacy, Cookies)

- effective / last updated date near the top
- who the policy applies to and what service it covers
- plain summary bullets optional; operative legal text must stand on its own
- contact method for legal/privacy inquiries
- standard disclaimer: informational only, not legal advice, subject to change where appropriate

## Hard bans

Align with `voice-and-boundaries.mdc`:
- No em dashes or en-dash pauses
- No AI slop or startup marketing tone
- No references to law firms, outside counsel, or drafting attribution
- No internal architecture, infra, or roadmap detail unless legally required and properly scoped

## Examples

```text
Bad:   We've got your back! By using our awesome platform, you're totally agreeing to these terms.
Good:  By accessing or using the Service, you agree to be bound by these Terms of Service.

Bad:   We might share data with partners when it makes sense for the product roadmap.
Good:  We may disclose Personal Information to service providers that perform services on our behalf, subject to contractual confidentiality obligations.

Bad:   Liability is basically zero (sorry!).
Good:  TO THE MAXIMUM EXTENT PERMITTED BY APPLICABLE LAW, IN NO EVENT SHALL COMPANY BE LIABLE FOR ANY INDIRECT, INCIDENTAL, SPECIAL, CONSEQUENTIAL, OR PUNITIVE DAMAGES.
```

## Process

1. Identify document type and audience (consumer policy vs. commercial agreement).
2. Outline required sections before drafting prose.
3. Draft definitions first; use them consistently throughout.
4. Read for ambiguity. Every obligation needs an actor, a duty, and a standard or timeframe.
5. Scan for marketing tone, em dashes, and missing standard protections.

When creating or revising legal documents, use this voice. Do not rewrite unrelated legal text outside the asked scope.
